{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Review remediation record (2026-08-21) for this ticket's plan — moved out of the description to fit the plan-review admission cap. What was factually wrong in the original plan and what was corrected:\n\n- FALSE CLAIM: \"the completion gate BYPASSES the ref resolver at `close_autoresume.py:141`\". It does not. Ref resolution is already uniform: every gate, the completion gate included, resolves through `gate_source.resolve_gate_handle` (`src/rebar/llm/gate_source.py:76`), the completion path reaching it at `src/rebar/llm/completion.py:149` via `close_autoresume.py:235`. Line 141 is `resolve_ref(ref or \"HEAD\", root, fetch=False)` inside `_reusable_attested_pass` — a cache-hit equality check comparing the close target against the signed `verified-at-sha`, not gate-source selection. Correction recorded as a constraint: routing THAT call through `resolve_gate_handle` would let the reuse check honour `REBAR_GATE_REF` and accept a certified PASS pinned to a DIFFERENT sha than the one being closed. What actually differs between gates is the arguments, not the resolver: the completion gate deliberately pins `source=\"attested\"`, `ref=(ref or \"HEAD\")`, `fetch=False` (epic `raze-vet-ditch` S4, rationale at `close_autoresume.py:225-234`) so the verdict is reproducible, branch-independent and signable; that pinning is load-bearing and unchanged by this work.\n\n- UNDERCOUNT: the plan said the CI \"this change predates the gate script\" shell guard appears twelve times. It appears 24 times across `.github/`: `_build-and-test.yml:145,159,181,195,210,288,377,570`; `_optionality.yml:108,114,133`; `_mutation.yml:64` (the 12 in scope); plus `.github/actions/docs-gates/action.yml` x10 and `gerrit-verify.yaml:323,332` (12 out of scope, which keep their inline guards). Bug `sapphire-vulnerable-fruitfly` added one of these guards and `anxious-resistant-urson` was a missing one.\n\n- MECHANISM GAP: the plan assumed a composite action could replace the inline guards directly. It cannot abort a caller's `run:` block — `uses:` cannot appear mid-`run:`, and a composite exiting 0 does not terminate the calling step. The design was changed to a guard step emitting a `present` output, with the existing work gated on `if: steps.<id>.outputs.present == 'true'`. Four sites carry behaviour a bare skip would silently drop and are now called out explicitly: `_mutation.yml:64` (also writes `matrix={\"shard\":[]}` and `has-shards=false` to `$GITHUB_OUTPUT` on skip, pinned by `tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py:250-267`); `_optionality.yml:108,114` (two generators guarded independently inside one step per bug `5bc6-5496-4e17-403a`); `_build-and-test.yml:181,195` (inverted `[ -f ]/else` with no `exit 0`, continuing afterwards); `_build-and-test.yml:377` (guards the DATA file `.github/git-version-floor.txt` via `$floorfile` and continues into a `sort -V` that can `exit 1`).\n\n- OVER-MERGE RISK: the plan implied `code_review_enabled` would be folded into `gate_enabled`. Recorded as a divergence that must be preserved — `code_review_enabled` (`src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py:277`) keeps its name, its `repo_root`-only signature, its broad `except Exception` and its silent `False` (`gate_dispatch.py:283-286`). Routing it through `gate_enabled` would narrow the catch to `ConfigError` (crashing code review instead of degrading it), add a stderr warning on every unreadable-config code review, and demand a `ticket_id` that code review, dispatching against a CHANGE rather than a ticket, has no source for.\n\n- TEST-SEAM HAZARD identified: 20 `monkeypatch.setattr(gate_dispatch, \"code_review_enabled\", ...)` sites across 6 files and 24 `monkeypatch.setattr(_gates, \"gate_enabled\", ...)` sites across 20 files bind those two symbols. If callers switch to calling `gate_policy.resolve(...)` directly, every one of those patches becomes inert but still green — the tests keep passing while exercising the real config. An acceptance criterion now pins that both symbols remain what production calls.\n\n- PATH CORRECTION: `test_close_gate_unreadable_config_2091.py` lives under `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/`, not `tests/unit/`; the pinning commands were fixed accordingly."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787371310049376001,"uuid":"84582c16-2be8-4c85-97ee-54a852273334"}